size would Fillhaus need to break even? How could it get there?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Basket needed: $210k ÷ (55 × 310 × 45%) = <b>$27.37</b>, 14% more than today.</p> <ul><li>Bundles of everyday staples (rice, oats, detergent) sold by the kilo</li><li>Click-and-collect refills prepared in advance (also saves staff time)</li> <li>Workplace orders: offices refilling cleaning products monthly</li><li>Higher-margin own-brand products</li></ul>"},{"q":"How would you negotiate with the landlord?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li><b>Turnover-based or stepped rent</b> for the first two years, so the landlord shares the footfall risk</li> <li><b>Fit-out contribution</b>, common for anchor-style tenants in a new development</li> <li><b>Break clause</b> after 24 months if footfall targets aren't met</li> <li>Ask for <b>footfall data</b> from the landlord's other centres to back up the 90 a day</li></ul>"}],"reco":"Don't sign on the current terms. The shop works only if the landlord's 90 customers a day is right ($91k profit); at the 55 a day Fillhaus's own shops achieve, it loses about $26k a year, with break-even at 63 customers. I recommend negotiating turnover-based rent and a fit-out contribution so the landlord shares the risk, and raising the basket towards $27 through bundles and workplace orders. Risk: a new development can take years to fill. Next step: request the landlord's footfall data from comparable centres.","great":["Questions the source of the forecast","Uses the client's own shops as a benchmark","Finds break-even in customers and in basket size","Turns the analysis into a negotiation"],"id":33,"slug":"fillhaus-refill"}